Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Period live-action adventure set in 1897. Humans and animals share a naturalistic, rugged visual style.

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S ESTATE - DAY

Autumn, 1897. In the sun-kissed Santa Clara Valley, BUCK rules a sprawling estate of lawns, orchards, stables, and a wide veranda.

Four years old and one hundred forty pounds, Buck is a broad-chested St. Bernard and Scotch shepherd mix. His dense, dark wolf-gray coat has brown at the muzzle and brows and a white blaze down his chest.

He plunges into the cement swimming tank, carries a laughing grandchild, hunts beside JUDGE MILLER'S sons, and settles at the Judge's feet.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was brewing. Men had found a yellow metal in the Arctic darkness, and the dogs they wanted were heavy dogs, strong of muscle and warm of coat.

Buck surveys his realm with the dignity of a king.

EXT. COLLEGE PARK FLAG STATION - NIGHT

MANUEL, a nervous gardener's helper in worn work clothes, leads Buck from the orchard to the lonely platform.

NED, a rough-coated kidnapper, passes Manuel folded money.

NED
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

Manuel doubles a stout rope beneath Buck's brass collar.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

The rope passes to Ned. Buck growls once, expecting obedience.

Ned jerks the rope tight. Buck springs, but Ned grapples his throat and throws him onto his back.

Buck's legs thrash. His tongue lolls. A locomotive shrieks into the station.

Manuel watches as Ned flings the unconscious Buck into the baggage car.

EXT. SEATTLE STOCKYARD - DAY

After two days without food or water, Buck rages inside a splintered crate. His eyes are bloodshot, his throat swollen.

MORGAN, a stout dog dealer in a sagging red sweater, pries away a board with a hatchet and raises a heavy club.

MORGAN
Now, you red-eyed devil.

Buck launches through the opening.

The club catches him in midair and smashes him down. Buck rises and charges again.

Another blow. Another fall. Buck attacks until a final strike leaves him senseless.

Later, Buck watches Morgan through half-closed eyes.

MORGAN
Well, Buck, my boy, we've had our little ruction. You've learned your place, and I know mine.

Morgan offers water. Buck drinks desperately, then takes raw meat from his hand.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was beaten, but he was not broken. A man with a club was a lawgiver, a master to be obeyed, though not necessarily conciliated.

PERRAULT, a small, weathered French-Canadian government courier, studies Buck.

PERRAULT
Sacredam! Dat one dam bully dog! Eh? How moch?

MORGAN
Three hundred, and a present at that.

Money changes hands. Perrault leads Buck and CURLY, a large, good-natured Newfoundland, toward the waterfront.

EXT. DYEA BEACH CAMP - DAY

Buck steps from a gangplank onto white ground. His paws sink. Snowflakes gather on his muzzle.

He snorts, licks the snow, and recoils when it melts on his tongue. Nearby men laugh.

Curly approaches a wolfish husky in friendship. Without warning, the husky slashes her face and leaps clear.

Curly rushes. The husky strikes her chest and topples her.

A silent circle of huskies closes over Curly. FRANCOIS, a broad, dark-skinned French-Canadian dog-driver, charges in with an axe, but Curly lies dead in the trampled snow.

SPITZ, a powerful snow-white husky, runs out his scarlet tongue as if laughing.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So that was the way. No fair play. Once down, that was the end of you. Buck would see to it that he never went down.

Francois buckles Buck into harness between solemn DAVE and one-eyed SOL-LEKS. Spitz waits at the head of the team.

FRANCOIS
Mush!

The sled jerks Buck forward into his new life.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

Spitz leads the team through deep snow. Dave nips Buck when he tangles the traces; Sol-leks shoulders him into place.

Buck learns to stop at "ho," move at "mush," swing wide on bends, and strain backward when the loaded sled descends.

At a halt, PIKE steals bacon while Perrault's back is turned. The following day, Buck steals a whole chunk and swallows it unseen.

At each night camp, Buck digs beneath the snow and curls into the warmth of his own body.

His soft paws harden. His shoulders thicken. He bites ice from between his toes and raises his muzzle to read the wind.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He watched and learned. The first theft marked him as fit to survive in the hostile Northland. The domesticated generations fell from him.

Spitz bares his teeth whenever Buck draws near. Buck returns a patient, deathless stare.

EXT. TAHKEENA CREEK - NIGHT

Under a white moon, a snowshoe rabbit races along the frozen creek. Buck leads a roaring pack behind it.

Spitz cuts across a bend and meets the rabbit head-on. His jaws break its back.

Buck drives into Spitz before the rabbit's cry has faded.

They circle in the moonlight while sixty huskies form a silent ring. Spitz slashes Buck's shoulder and leaps clear.

Buck rushes again and again, streaming blood, unable to penetrate Spitz's guard.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck fought by instinct, but he could fight by head as well.

Buck feints for the throat, sweeps low, and clamps onto Spitz's left foreleg.

Bone crunches.

Spitz struggles on three legs. Buck repeats the trick on the right foreleg.

The white dog staggers as the waiting circle tightens.

Buck springs in and drives shoulder against shoulder. Spitz falls beneath the dark mass of huskies.

Buck stands alone in the moonlight, the successful champion.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL CAMP - DAWN

Francois finds Buck covered with wounds and Spitz gone.

FRANCOIS
Eh? Wot I say? I spik true w'en I say dat Buck two devils.

PERRAULT
Dat Spitz fight lak hell.

FRANCOIS
An' dat Buck fight lak two hells.

Francois places Sol-leks at the head of the harness. Buck drives him aside and claims the position.

Francois advances with a club. Buck retreats beyond its reach but refuses his old place.

Perrault watches precious minutes pass.

PERRAULT
T'row down de club.

Francois drops it. Buck trots in triumphantly and swings into the lead.

The team launches. Under Buck's command, the dogs leap as one, flying up the Yukon at record pace.

EXT. SKAGUAY CAMP - DAY

Five months and twenty-five hundred miles later, Buck weighs only one hundred fifteen pounds. His coat hangs dull over prominent ribs.

A government agent sells the exhausted team to three newcomers.

HAL is nineteen, beardless, and burdened with a cartridge belt, revolver, and hunting knife. CHARLES is middle-aged, with watery eyes and a fiercely curled mustache. MERCEDES, Charles's soft and fashionably dressed wife, is Hal's sister.

They build a mountainous sled load of clothes, canned goods, blankets, dishes, and a bulky tent. Fourteen dogs strain before it.

HAL
Mush on there!

The frozen sled does not move. Hal lashes the dogs.

MERCEDES
Oh, Hal, you mustn't! The poor dears!

HAL
They're lazy, I tell you, and you've got to whip them to get anything out of them.

Charles finally rocks the runners free. The sled lurches forward, overturns at the first bend, and scatters their possessions down Skaguay's main street.

Hal falls and is dragged behind it.

Silent townspeople catch the dogs and discard half the load. The next morning, Buck leads the disorderly outfit north.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

The newcomers take half a day to break camp and make scarcely ten miles before dark.

At first they overfeed the dogs. Soon the food sacks stand empty.

Fourteen traces dwindle to five. Empty collars knock against the sled.

BILLEE collapses and cannot rise. Hal lifts an axe. Buck turns away before the blow falls.

Mercedes rides atop the load while Charles stumbles behind. Hal's club drives the starving dogs onward.

Buck is a walking skeleton, his once-glorious coat matted with dried blood. He pulls until he falls, rises under blows, and pulls again.

Spring sunlight floods the valley. Meltwater runs beneath thinning Yukon ice, opening black fissures ahead of the sled.

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

JOHN THORNTON, a weathered prospector in a dark wool coat and fur cap, whittles an axe handle beside the river.

The five dogs stagger into camp and collapse. Hal points toward the rotten ice.

HAL
They told us up above that the bottom was dropping out of the trail. They told us we couldn't make White River, and here we are.

JOHN THORNTON
And they told you true. Only fools, with the blind luck of fools, could have made it. I wouldn't risk my carcass on that ice for all the gold in Alaska.

HAL
All the same, we'll go on to Dawson. Get up there, Buck! Mush on!

Sol-leks, Pike, Joe, and Teek struggle upright. Buck remains down.

Hal whips him, then replaces the whip with his club. Blow after blow falls on Buck's unresisting body.

Thornton springs forward and hurls Hal into the snow.

JOHN THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
It's my dog. Get out of my way, or I'll fix you.

Hal draws his hunting knife. Thornton smashes his knuckles with the axe handle, takes the knife, and cuts Buck's traces.

Hal, Charles, and Mercedes depart with the remaining dogs. Thornton kneels beside Buck as the sled crawls onto the river.

The back of the sled drops. Hal and the gee-pole jerk upward.

Mercedes screams.

A whole section of ice gives way. Humans, dogs, and sled vanish into the black water.

Thornton and Buck face one another.

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks his hand.

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

Weeks later, Buck sleeps in spring sunshine while SKEET, a small red Irish setter, cleans his healing wounds. NIG, a huge black half-bloodhound and half-deerhound, rests beside him.

Buck's ribs disappear beneath new muscle. His dark coat regains its silken sheen.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time. It had taken John Thornton to arouse it.

Thornton takes Buck's head between his rough hands, rests cheek against cheek, and shakes him lovingly.

Buck seizes Thornton's hand in his jaws, presses down without breaking the skin, then slowly releases it.

JOHN THORNTON
God! You can all but speak!

Buck settles at Thornton's feet, his eyes fixed upon the man who saved him.

EXT. ELDORADO SALOON AND DAWSON MAIN STREET - DAY

A sled loaded with twenty sacks of flour stands frozen to the hard-packed snow. Miners crowd around it.

MATTHEWSON, a prosperous Bonanza king, faces Thornton beside the load.

JOHN THORNTON
Buck can start a thousand pounds.

MATTHEWSON
And break it out? And walk off with it for a hundred yards?

JOHN THORNTON
And break it out, and walk off with it for a hundred yards.

MATTHEWSON
I've got a thousand dollars that says he can't.

Thornton turns to his old friend JIM O'BRIEN.

JOHN THORNTON
Can you lend me a thousand?

JIM O'BRIEN
Sure. Though it's little faith I'm having, John, that the beast can do the trick.

Buck is harnessed alone to the sled. Thornton kneels and rests his cheek against Buck's.

JOHN THORNTON
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck gently takes Thornton's mittened hand, then releases it.

Thornton steps back.

JOHN THORNTON
Gee!

Buck lunges right. The frozen runners crack.

JOHN THORNTON
Haw!

Buck lunges left. The sled pivots several inches.

JOHN THORNTON
Now, MUSH!

Buck throws himself forward. His chest drops near the ground, muscles knotting beneath his coat.

One foot slips. The crowd groans.

The sled moves half an inch, then an inch. It lurches into steady motion.

Buck hauls the thousand-pound load past the marker one hundred yards away.

The street erupts. Thornton falls to his knees, embraces Buck, and curses him softly and lovingly.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck earned sixteen hundred dollars in five minutes for John Thornton.

EXT. LOST MINE CAMP - DAY

Months of trackless travel bring Thornton, Buck, PETE, a bearded woodsman, and HANS, a compact German prospector, into a broad eastern valley.

Gold lies across the bottom of their washing pan like yellow butter.

Moose-hide sacks accumulate beside a spruce-bough lodge. With little work to do, Buck lies near the fire and stares into the forest.

JOHN THORNTON
Never was there such a dog.

PETE
When he was made, the mould was broke.

HANS
Py Jingo! I t'ink so mineself.

A long, distant wolf howl drifts from the trees.

Buck rises. His mane ripples. He slips away from the fire.

EXT. FOREST CREEK - NIGHT

Buck enters a moonlit clearing and finds a long, lean TIMBER WOLF howling at the sky.

The wolf flees. Buck overtakes and corners him, but makes no attack.

They circle, sniff noses, then play with nervous, half-savage joy.

The wolf leads Buck through the forest. They run side by side for hours, answering the call.

By dawn, they stop at a stream. Buck drinks, then looks back toward Thornton's distant camp.

The wolf urges him onward. Buck turns for home.

The wild brother follows for a time, whining softly, then sits and sends a mournful howl after him.

EXT. MOOSE VALLEY - DAY

Autumn. Buck, now one hundred fifty pounds and more wolf than dog in movement, stalks a herd of moose.

A great bull carries a feathered arrowhead in its flank.

Buck cuts the wounded bull from the herd. He feints beneath the antlers, drives it from water, and denies it food or rest.

For four days, the pursuit continues beneath changing skies.

At last the bull drops. Buck pulls it down and feeds.

A day later, Buck lifts his blood-darkened muzzle. Strange human scent rides the wind from the direction of camp.

He races toward Thornton.

EXT. LOST MINE CAMP - DAY

Buck finds Nig dead in a thicket, an arrow through his body.

Farther on, Hans lies face-down, feathered with arrows.

A sing-song chant rises from the ruined camp. YEEHAT HUNTERS dance around the wreckage of the spruce-bough lodge.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For the last time in his life, Buck allowed passion to usurp cunning and reason. It was because of his great love for John Thornton that he lost his head.

Buck erupts into the clearing with a terrible roar.

He tears open the YEEHAT CHIEF'S throat, strikes a second hunter, and bounds through the shower of arrows.

An arrow meant for Buck strikes another hunter. The survivors scatter into the forest.

Buck pursues, dragging down the nearest, then returns to the desolated camp.

Pete lies dead in his blankets. Skeet rests beside a muddy pool, faithful to the last.

Buck follows Thornton's scent to the water. No trace leads away.

He waits at the edge, listening for a voice that never comes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Death, as a passing out and away from the lives of the living, he knew. And he knew John Thornton was dead. The last tie was broken.

EXT. LOST MINE CAMP - NIGHT

A full moon rises over the ruined lodge.

Sharp yelps gather in the forest. A wolf pack pours into the clearing like a silvery flood.

Buck stands motionless before them.

The boldest wolf leaps. Buck breaks its neck with one flashing strike.

Three more attack and retreat bleeding. The pack draws back, wary and awed.

The long, lean wild brother advances and whines softly. Buck recognizes him, and they touch noses.

An old, battle-scarred wolf sits beneath the moon and howls. The whole pack joins him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was the call, the many-noted call, sounding more luringly and compellingly than ever before. And as never before, Buck was ready to obey.

Buck raises his muzzle and adds his great voice to the chorus.

The leaders spring into the trees. Buck runs with them, side by side with the wild brother.

EXT. LOST MINE CAMP - NIGHT

Years later. The camp has vanished beneath grass and snow.

A wolf pack crosses the valley in pale moonlight. Several wolves bear brown upon their heads and muzzles and white rifts down their chests.

Buck runs at their head, gigantic above his fellows, his dark coat glimmering beneath the aurora.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Yeehats tell of a Ghost Dog that runs at the head of the pack. When the long winter nights come on, he may be seen leaping above his fellows, singing a song of the younger world, which is the song of the pack.

Buck's howl rolls across the frozen wilderness. The pack answers as one.

> FADE OUT.

> THE END <
